PR #2782  overall: bug_confirmed
Spec: SSPUSH/ssp store and ssp update lack mutual ordering — multi-observer TOCTOU race on shadow stack state without explicit fences.
  [BUG] arch/riscv/kernel/usercfi.c:182-200  save_user_shstk
      create_rstor_token (line 192) performs ssamoswap.d to write a restore token to the shadow stack, then set_active_shstk (line 196) immediately updates the in-memory ssp to point to that token location — with zero FENCE instructions between them. A cross-hart observer (ptrace, IPI backtrace, /proc) reading the in-memory ssp will see ssp pointing to the token location before the ssamoswap.d store is globally visible, meaning the observer sees stale/uninitialized shadow stack content at the claimed ssp.
  [BUG] arch/riscv/kernel/usercfi.c:206-228  restore_user_shstk
      amo_user_shstk (line 210) performs ssamoswap.d to clear the restore token, then set_active_shstk (line 226) updates the in-memory ssp to the token's target address — with zero FENCE instructions between them. Two inconsistent states are observable cross-hart: (a) ssp updated to new location before the old token's clear is globally visible, and (b) old token cleared but ssp still at old location as read from a stale cache line.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/usercfi.c:129-152  amo_user_shstk
      This is the core ssamoswap.d primitive. The inline asm encodes ssamoswap.d without aq/rl bits, meaning it has neither acquire nor release semantics. It is the sole ordering primitive for all shadow stack token writes. Every caller (create_rstor_token, restore_user_shstk) proceeds to update in-memory ssp immediately after this call without inserting a FENCE. The missing FENCE after this amoswap is the root cause of every cross-hart visibility gap.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/entry.S:99-110  save_userssp
      csrrw reads CSR_SSP into a GPR then REG_S stores it to thread_info (TASK_TI_USER_SSP). No FENCE follows the store, and no FENCE precedes the csrrw. The in-memory ssp copy is in a location accessible to other harts. The CSR_SSP value reflects the hart's local SSP state, which may have been advanced by userspace SSPUSH instructions whose shadow stack memory stores are not yet globally visible. A remote hart reading this in-memory ssp via IPI_CPU_BACKTRACE or kgdb will observe ssp that points ahead of globally-visible shadow stack content.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/entry.S:112-123  restore_userssp
      REG_L loads ssp from thread_info, then csrw writes CSR_SSP. No FENCE precedes the REG_L. After a context-switch, the restored ssp may reference shadow stack entries originally written by SSPUSH on a prior hart — entries that are not guaranteed globally visible on the current hart. The correctness depends on the implicit invariant that all prior stores (including shadow stack stores from the previous hart) are fenced by the context-switch path, but this is not locally verifiable in the macro.
  [BUG] arch/riscv/kernel/signal.c:145-172  save_cfiss_state
      Calls save_user_shstk (which has the ssamoswap→set_active_shstk FENCE gap documented above), then immediately copies the resulting ss_ptr to userspace sigframe via __put_user (line 167). No FENCE separates save_user_shstk from the userspace copyout. A concurrent reader (/proc/pid/mem, another thread sharing the mm, a ptrace debugger on a different hart) can observe the sigframe's ss_ptr value before the corresponding restore token written by create_rstor_token's ssamoswap.d is globally visible in shadow stack memory.
  [BUG] arch/riscv/kernel/signal.c:174-199  __restore_cfiss_state
      Reads ss_ptr from userspace sigframe via __copy_from_user (line 193), then calls restore_user_shstk (line 198) which does ssamoswap→set_active_shstk without FENCE. The inherited ordering gap means that between the ssamoswap token clear and the set_active_shstk, an NMI/IPI backtrace on a remote hart reading CSR_SSP (via the in-memory copy) could observe ssp pointing to the already-cleared token before the clear is globally visible.
  [BUG] arch/riscv/kernel/ptrace.c:295-322  riscv_cfi_get
      Reads shstk_ptr via get_active_shstk(target) at line 318, where target is a potentially running task on a remote hart. Returns this in-memory ssp value to the ptrace observer (userspace debugger) via membuf_write with zero FENCE ordering. This is the exact cross-hart observation pattern the spec warns about: ptrace sees ssp before the last SSPUSH store on the traced hart's shadow stack is globally visible, exposing stale shadow stack content at the reported ssp.
  [BUG] arch/riscv/kernel/ptrace.c:331-371  riscv_cfi_set
      Writes new ssp via set_active_shstk(target, user_cfi.shstk_ptr) at line 369 for a task potentially running on another hart. Zero FENCE follows the store. A debugger pivoting the shadow stack (setting ssp to a new location) has no guarantee that shadow stack entries at the new ssp are globally visible — the debugger may observe torn or stale shadow stack content, and the target hart may resume with ssp pointing to entries not yet coherent.
  [BUG] arch/riscv/kernel/smp.c:158-163  handle_IPI
      IPI_CPU_BACKTRACE (line 158-159) calls nmi_cpu_backtrace() which reads the interrupted hart's saved ssp from thread_info — a cross-hart read without any FENCE ordering guarantee. IPI_KGDB_ROUNDUP (line 161-162) calls kgdb_nmicallback(cpu, get_irq_regs()) which also reads ssp cross-hart. Both paths consume the in-memory ssp value saved by save_userssp without any barrier ensuring that userspace SSPUSH stores on the interrupted hart are globally visible to the IPI-receiving hart.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/process.c:86-101  __show_regs
      Prints ssp via get_active_shstk(current) at line 97. When called from the local hart (die(), show_regs() on the crashing hart), this is safe — it reads the hart's own in-memory ssp copy. However, __show_regs is also reachable via IPI_CPU_BACKTRACE (nmi_cpu_backtrace → show_regs → __show_regs), where current is the interrupted remote hart. In that cross-hart context, the ssp read inherits the ordering gap from the IPI path described in handle_IPI: the printed ssp may not match globally-visible shadow stack content.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/usercfi.c:158-176  create_rstor_token
      Performs ssamoswap.d via amo_user_shstk (line 169) to write a self-referencing restore token. No FENCE follows. As a standalone function, the token store may not be globally visible when a subsequent observer sees ssp updated to point here. This is always called in sequence with a set_active_shstk (in save_user_shstk at line 196, or allocate_shadow_stack at line 248-252 followed by set_active_shstk at the call site). The risk is realized through the callers' lack of FENCE before the ssp update.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/usercfi.c:52-55  set_active_shstk
      Bare memory write to task->thread_info.user_cfi_state.user_shdw_stk. This function is the mechanism by which ssp becomes visible cross-hart. Every caller pairs it with a preceding shadow stack memory operation (ssamoswap.d), and zero callers insert a FENCE between the shadow stack store and this in-memory ssp update. The function itself is trivial; the vulnerability is the systemic absence of FENCE at every call site.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/usercfi.c:57-60  get_active_shstk
      Bare memory read from task->thread_info.user_cfi_state.user_shdw_stk. Used by ptrace (cross-hart read of remote task's ssp), __show_regs (potentially cross-hart via IPI backtrace), save_user_shstk, and restore_user_shstk. The reader has no FENCE to ensure that the shadow stack content at the returned ssp is globally visible. This is the reader-side of the missing-FENCE pair.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/usercfi.c:62-75  set_shstk_status
      Modifies the in-memory ubcfi_en flag (line 67), then updates task->thread.envcfg (lines 69-72), then writes CSR_ENVCFG (line 74). No FENCE. The CSR_ENVCFG write enables/disables SSE (Shadow Stack Enable in senvcfg), which gates how SSPUSH/SSPOPCHK behave. This CSR write takes effect locally on this hart, but the in-memory ubcfi_en flag (read cross-hart by ptrace and KVM) may be observed by another hart before the CSR_ENVCFG write takes architectural effect. The invariant 'all harts observe both the ubcfi_en flag and the SSE CSR state consistently when SSE transitions' is NOT enforced.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/usercfi.c:230-255  allocate_shadow_stack
      Allocates shadow stack VMA with VM_SHADOW_STACK (line 241-242). When set_tok=true (line 245-252), calls create_rstor_token which performs ssamoswap.d without FENCE. The caller (shstk_alloc_thread_stack at line 348, arch_set_shadow_stack_status at line 423) then immediately sets active ssp. The token store is not ordered with respect to the ssp becoming globally visible.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/usercfi.c:303-349  shstk_alloc_thread_stack
      Allocates new shadow stack for clone/fork (line 342). Returns addr+size to the caller (copy_thread), where the ssp is set via set_active_shstk at line 275. The ssp value is exposed to the parent task via /proc and ptrace immediately after copy_thread returns — but the child's shadow stack initialization (allocation + any token writes) may not be globally visible on other harts at that point. Depends on the implicit invariant 'fork() synchronization ensures child's shadow stack is globally coherent before parent observes child's ssp', which is not enforced by any FENCE in the shadow stack initialization path.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/process.c:263-275  copy_thread
      Calls shstk_alloc_thread_stack (line 264) then sets the child's active ssp via set_active_shstk at line 275. No FENCE between the shadow stack allocation (+ optional token write) and the ssp store. The child's ssp is visible to the parent via /proc/pid/mem and ptrace from the moment copy_thread completes. There is no guarantee that the child's shadow stack initial content is globally coherent at that point.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/entry.S:180-194  handle_exception
      Kernel entry point calling save_userssp at line 184. This is where ssp transitions from CSR_SSP (hart-local) to in-memory thread_info (cross-hart observable). The save_userssp call itself has no FENCE, and the handle_exception path has no shadow-stack-specific fence at any point. From this instruction forward, ssp is in shared memory and potentially stale relative to userspace SSPUSH stores on this hart.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/crash_save_regs.S:13-56  riscv_crash_save_regs
      Saves all GP registers (x1-x31) plus STATUS, TVAL, CAUSE, and EPC (via auipc for the crash PC), but does NOT save CSR_SSP. In vmcore/crash dump analysis, the shadow stack pointer is lost entirely — the analyst cannot correlate the crashed hart's register state with its shadow stack position. Combined with the cross-hart ordering issue, even if ssp were saved, there would be no guarantee that shadow stack memory is consistent with the crashing hart's last SSPUSH. The omission of ssp from crash dumps removes the only forensic artifact that could reveal a shadow stack corruption.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/vdso_cfi/Makefile:1-26  N/A (build configuration)
      The VDSO CFI build compiles vDSO code with -fcf-protection=full (line 22), which causes the compiler to emit SSPUSH/SSPOPCHK in function prologues/epilogues. Between successive VDSO calls from different harts, earlier SSPUSH stores from hart A may not be globally visible when hart B's sspopchk reads the shadow stack. The kernel controls this image and its CFI instrumentation, but the cross-hart ordering of SSPUSH→shadow stack store vs. sspopchk→shadow stack load is not architecturally guaranteed without FENCE. This is inherent in the ISA memory model — the kernel cannot fix this with software alone, but it is a latent cross-hart race.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/traps.c:374-398  handle_user_cfi_violation
      For BCFI violations (sspopchk mismatch, tval=3), delivers SIGSEGV with SEGV_CPERR (line 392-393). If another hart concurrently clears the restore token being checked (via restore_user_shstk's ssamoswap), the ordering between the remote hart's ssamoswap and the local hart's sspopchk is architecturally not guaranteed atomic. The kernel cannot distinguish a genuine shadow stack corruption (attack) from a cross-hart token invalidation race (legitimate kernel operation on another hart sharing the same mm). Both produce the same tval and the same fatal signal.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/traps.c:409-423  do_trap_software_check
      Dispatches to handle_user_cfi_violation for user-mode traps (line 415). The trap handler itself observes the local hart's consistent architectural view. However, a cross-hart race on shadow stack tokens (another hart clearing/rewriting a token concurrent with this hart's sspopchk) can cause the software check exception without an actual CFI policy violation. The handler treats all BCFI tval=3 exceptions identically — there is no mechanism to detect or log a potential cross-hart race vs. a true attack.
