  [RISK] contrib/llvm-project/llvm/lib/Target/RISCV/RISCVInstrInfoZicfiss.td:27-48  (tablegen — SSPUSH/SSPOPCHK/SSRDP instruction definitions)
      SSPUSH is modeled with hasSideEffects=0, mayStore=1, Uses=[SSP], Defs=[SSP]. The read-modify-write chain on SSP prevents the compiler from reordering two SSPUSH instructions relative to each other (true data dependency), so successive-SSPUSH ordering is implicitly enforced by the Uses/Defs chain, not by an explicit fence. However, hasSideEffects=0 means the compiler may freely reorder SSPUSH relative to unrelated stores/loads — other harts or debug monitors observing ssp (via SSRDP) and then reading shadow-stack memory could see updated-ssp pointing to a location where the store is not yet globally visible, because the RISC-V memory model does not order ssp updates with respect to the shadow-stack store. This is currently benign in practice because (a) frame lowering emits only a single SSPUSH for RA, and (b) cross-hart shadow-stack observation is abnormal. Risk materialises if a future pass emits multiple SSPUSH without an intervening FENCE and depends on cross-hart observability.
